  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13928579/assets/fd233e2f5214cdc5a27cffa20ccb7ddcfc8ff089963c4381363edabd328ace5c.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials could be the function shown in the diagram above?",
  "worked_solution": "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even,\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative, and\n\n- the $y$-intercept is $1,$ which means that $P(0)=1.$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the only one that satisfies all three criteria is $y=-{x}^{6}-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-x+1.$",
